<h1>compact DAWG</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="directAcycGraph.html"><em>directed acyclic</em></a> word graph (DAWG) representing the <a href="suffix.html"><em>suffixes</em></a> of a given <a href="string.html"><em>string</em></a> in which each <a href="edge.html"><em>edge</em></a> is labeled with the longest possible string.  The strings along a <a href="path.html"><em>path</em></a> from the <a href="root.html"><em>root</em></a> to a <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> are the <a href="substring.html"><em>substring</em></a> which the <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> represents.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="directedAcyclicWordGraph.html"><em>directed acyclic word graph</em></a>, <a href="patriciatree.html"><em>Patricia tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
This is a variant of a directed acyclic word graph, or DAWG, in which a <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> with one <a href="child.html"><em>child</em></a> is merged with its <a href="parent.html"><em>parent</em></a> and the edge labels are concatenated.  A Patricia tree merges single-child nodes also, but does not combine common subtrees.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Andrew W. Appel and Guy J. Jacobson</strong>, <em>The World's Fastest Scrabble Program</em>, CACM, 31(5):572-578, May 1988. Good description of the basic DAWG.
</p>

